# Review: exponential and unbounded cost

The floor for new map, cache, and GLES work is a Cubieboard 2 or a
Raspberry Pi 3: about 1 GB of RAM shared with the system, a GLES2 GPU
(Mali-400, VideoCore IV), and `IsSlowCPU()` (at most 1.4 GHz). Kobo is
a separate floor: software canvas and e-paper refresh, no GPU pipeline.
Cost that grows with waypoints, airspaces, traffic, NOTAM, or tiles
will stall flight. Flag algorithmic blow-ups, not micro-optimisations
of cold setup code.

## Hot paths (must stay cheap)

- MergeThread (~50 ms): cheap merges only
- CalculationThread (≤ 2 Hz): no nested scans of unbounded sets
- Map paint / device NMEA parse / UI event handlers

## Reject

- Nested loops over two growing collections (waypoints × airspaces,
  traffic × airspace, tiles × time steps) on a hot path
- Recursion without a hard depth or size bound
- Unbounded caches, maps, or overlay lists, and any cache whose
  resident size you cannot state in megabytes. Cap it, evict, and put
  the byte count in the comment (as `MAX_CACHED_TILES` does)
- On the map paint path, a draw call, texture bind, or texture upload
  inside a loop over a growing set (waypoints, trail points, tiles,
  airspaces). Batch as `TrailRenderer` does
- Retries, downloads, or timers that can pile up without a cap
- Combinatorial search (task points × waypoints × routes) without the
  existing Engine path/route solvers
- Heavy templates that explode compile time for one call site

```cpp
// BAD: O(n*m) every calculation tick
for (const auto &wp : waypoints)
  for (const auto &as : *airspaces)
    if (as.Inside(wp.location)) ...

// GOOD: spatial query, or run only when the set changed

// BAD: one draw per waypoint, every frame
for (const auto &wp : waypoints)
  canvas.DrawLine(wp.a, wp.b);

// GOOD: one batched draw for the set, or skip until the set changes
```

Prefer the existing Engine indexes and solvers. If the input can grow
with a long flight or a large CUP/airspace file, state the complexity.
